[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4cbb6e4f-4794-5cda-aa3c-d8ef001e7457",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-\\frac{5}{3}|-3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (\\frac{4}{3},2]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{3})$\n- **III.** $x\\in (1,\\frac{5}{3}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{5}{3}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(\\frac{4}{3},2]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{11}{6}$, both with output $- \\frac{17}{6}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{3})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{5}{3}\\ge \\frac{5}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(1,\\frac{5}{3}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{5}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{3}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b97630c0-b290-5dbd-a04b-db36a0c40c0b",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
