    "question": {
      ":question/id": "be263055-0cc6-5b4a-a767-e208f02dcb31",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{4}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2})$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{3}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(\\frac{1}{2},1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{5}{8}$ and $\\frac{7}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{65}{64}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{4}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{3}{4}\\ge \\frac{3}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[0,\\frac{1}{2})$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{2}\\le \\frac{3}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
